\f1\fs22 Since Eloise cut short her interview with Sarah before the attack on the mansion and the subsequent escape to Eloise\'92s compound in the mountains, Hayley\'92s questions for Sarah had multiplied. And she was impatient for answers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 While Sarah was out chatting with Eloise, Hayley showered, brewed camomile tea and claimed the most luxurious sofa in the townhouse. Through the French doors: manicured native shrubs, artfully placed flowers, glimpses of the street beyond. The morning was cold enough to make the colours burst.\
As Hayley sipped her tea and meditated on the view, a plan for interviewing Sarah crystallised. With confidence growing, she relaxed and allowed her mind to wander.\
To other people.\
Kathy. Hayley\'92s eyes glinted as she applied her smile to her cup and savoured the last of the tea. She crossed her legs and felt a frisson of delight.\
Kathy: so inexplicable. Earlier, while brewing the tea, Hayley had watched Kathy in the back yard\'97rolling on the grass, arms thrown skyward in what looked like ecstatic devotion. Shamanic. Doctor Lanning was some kind of witch, living between science and atavistic tradition.\
Hayley liked that. She wished she could be as free as Kathy appeared to be. 
\f2\i Bullshit
\f1\i0 , she corrected herself. 
\f2\i You need order. Structure. That\'92s who you are
\f1\i0 . And true\'97that\'92s what she sought to do all her life; in the chaotic jeopardy of her childhood home, and in every relationship since. It was why she joined TRAMPS, aware she would need to actively resist the axiomatic corruption in it.\
Hayley toyed with the idea of incorporating a little more Kathy-ness in her life.\
Glimpses of movement of the street brought Hayley out of reverie. Steps on the gravel path. The front door clicked open and shortly shut with gentler percussion. The hall closet made similar sounds too\'97Sarah hanging up the coat she borrowed to go outside.\
\'93Oh, hi!\'94 Sarah said, surprised to see Hayley in the front room. \'93Everything good?\'94\
Hayley said nothing for a few moments. \'93I was wondering if we could pick up where Eloise interrupted us.\'94\
Sarah stiffened. \'93You mean your interrogation.\'94\
Hayley let silence rest between them. \'93We don\'92t know if this is sanctuary or prison. We could pool information. We could be allies.\'94\
Sarah exhaled, and her shoulders regained some of their suppleness. \'93Okay. Shall I get Kathy?\'94\
In spite of the sofa being designed for curling up in, Hayley tried sitting with a straighter back. \'93Just us for now, okay?\'94\
Sarah noticed the way Hayley held her tea cup; cradling in one hand while gripping it tighter in the other, like she was choking it. \'93What are you drinking?\'94\
\'93Oh, camomile. The pot\'92s in the kitchen. You may still get a good brew if you add more water.\'94\
\'93Sure. Like some more?\'94 Sarah extended her hand to accept Hayley\'92s cup.\
\'93Please.\'94\
When Sarah returned and settled on the sofa alongside her, Hayley allowed a few drawn-out moments while each tasted and reflected on the brew in the cups. \'93You and Tony Chantara: you seem like an unlikely match,\'94 she said without taking her eyes off her tea.\
\'93Oh we\'92re not,\'94 Sarah said congruently, and with some amusement. \'93We\'92re not any kind of match.\'94\
\'93Do you often buy underwear for people you are not in relationship with?\'94\
Sarah laughed and had to put her cup on the table. \'93Oh I was intending to tease him with them.\'94 And then chilled. \'93You\'92ve been in my apartment.\'94\
Hayley allowed a moment, then: \'93How were you intending to tease him with them?\'94\
More wary, Sarah paused, while she rehearsed her answer. \'93The first time I met him, he just looked so pathetic, and a little bit adorable\'85 he was trying to hide a rip in his overalls from me, which was hard because the lurid pink of his underwear couldn\'92t have been brighter than if he had a lighthouse in his pants.\'94\
\'93Do you often encounter men who have lighthouses in their pants?\'94 Hayley smiled over her tea cup, and slid a quick glance Sarah\'92s way. \'93When was that, the first time you met?\'94\
\'93It was the first day at my new job\'85 awful, awful\'85 one of the execs there had \'91retired\'92, as they like to say\'85 shot himself in the head with a shotgun.\'94\
\'93You\'92re referring to Travis Teh?\'94\
\'93Yes, I am.\'94\
\'93The first time you met Tony Chantara was the day Teh died?\'94\
\'93Yes, he\'92d come to clean the office.\'94\
\'93And you\'92d never met before\'85\'94\
\'93No. Why?\'94\
\'93That\'92s strange.\'94 Hayley kept her eyes on the flowers in the garden. Peripheral vision caught more truth than direct staring\'97the body told stories the face tried to hide. Here it comes, she thought, and sipped tea.\
\'93You\'92re about to ask why I met up with Tony later that day,\'94 Sarah said, deciding to get ahead of the inconsistency.\
\'93At the Stickett Inn,\'94 Hayley laid out for Sarah.\
\'93Yes,\'94 Sarah said with a fine, anxious crack in her voice.\
\'93He\'92s a regular there,\'94 Hayley stated. \'93I spoke to his drinking buddies.\'94\
\'93Oh?\'94 Sarah found it difficult to find a comfortable way to sit on the sofa.\
\'93They said you were only there a few minutes, until you whisked him away for a dinner date.\'94\
Sarah hesitated momentarily, searching for what to say next. Hayley noticed; it should have been an easy segue from Agent Jones visiting Sarah to needing to warn Chantara of the danger, though how an Airborne woman would care enough about a Cloudborne grunt she\'92d only met hours before\'85 in all her years of investigating crime Hayley had never met an Airborne of Sarah\'92s station (as evidenced by her undeniable attractiveness and therefore the quality of her lineage) who cared about anyone but themselves.\
\'93The dinner date was a cover to get Chantara away from his friends so you could warn him that a PAPER agent was looking for him,\'94 Hayley prompted, to give Sarah another opportunity.\
\'93Yes, that\'92s right,\'94 Sarah said, but it didn\'92t ring true to Hayley.\
Hayley looked away towards the garden. Sarah was breathing deeper than she was moments ago. There was something else going on here, so Hayley put her planned line of enquiry aside while she pursued another thread. \'93Tell me about Chantara\'92s friends.\'94 \
Sarah\'92s unblinking stare was readable even from the corner of Hayley\'92s eye. When Hayley looked at her directly, for the first time since she\'92d sat down, Sarah aligned herself to projecting a calmer image.\
\'93There were three of them. I don\'92t recall their names; they were just barflies, the kind of drinking buddies you\'92d expect a cleaner to drink with.\'94\
Hayley allowed silence to rest while she held Sarah\'92s gaze, which was as good as asking, 
\f2\i All three of them?
\f1\i0 \
Sarah held herself rigid for as long as she could endure.\
\'93Hey,\'94 Hayley offered, and put a friendly hand on Sarah\'92s knee. \'93What\'92s got you so spooked?\'94\
Sarah shook her head. Then she stared at Hayley, appraising her.\
Hayley gently massaged Sarah\'92s thigh. \'93You can trust me.\'94\
Sarah processed her thoughts for several seconds while Hayley held her gaze and waited for something to shift. When the corner of Sarah\'92s mouth twitched, Hayley nodded and smiled. Sarah smiled a little too, and nodded momentarily in time with Hayley.\
\'93One of them is an undercover agent of some kind\'85 PAPER, I thought\'85 I hope.\'94\
\'93Hey, even if he\'92s a TRAMP, I\'92m on your side, okay?\'94\
Sarah nodded uncertainly and looked down at the rug under the coffee table.\
\'93Which of them did you suspect?\'94 Hayley asked, not risking revealing she already knew who it would be.\
\'93His name is Halo.\'94\
\'93The pick-up artist, Haloren Smythe.\'94\
\'93You interviewed him?\'94\
\'93I tried.\'94 Hayley hesitated. \'93Nearly got my arse shot off by a PAPER gunship.\'94\
Sarah exhaled and nodded herself to a more animated state. \'93I knew he was PAPER, but Tony told me they\'92d been friends for years.\'94\
\'93Interesting. What made you suspect him of being an undercover operative?\'94\
\'93He\'92s got empathy tech, and not just the consumer-grade stuff that creepy philanthropists buy. His is the high-grade stuff only agencies have access to.\'94\
Hayley wondered how Sarah would know about empathy tech. \'93That\'92s very interesting.\'94 She was feeling both the triumph of reaffirming her theory that Halo was the Scarlet Shower Surgeon, and the frustration that she was imprisoned while he was roaming free. \'93Let\'92s return to Agent Jones. Why is he so sure you and Chantara have stolen state secrets?\'94\
Sarah went to the French doors and stared out at nothing, her back to Hayley. The memory hit\'97Tony\'92s van tipping, careening into the kebab cart. The disc shimmering in the footwell. Her skin crawled as her flesh recalled the somatic threat of the sticky, slavering thing fusing into her. Her heart thumped with fear of exposure and existential terror\'97the Gyges Disc had imbued Tony with apparently supernatural abilities to regenerate. Sarah clamped her eyes closed against vivid images of Tony being tortured by PAPER. And, through it all, Tony had never given her up. Dear Mr Fairy Boxers, who knew a Cloudborne could exhibit so much courage? And what had she done?\'97she\'92d handed over the disc at the first threat of danger. 
\f2\i This is not me
\f1\i0 , she reflected critically. 
\f2\i I\'92m stronger than this.
\f1\i0  But maybe she wasn\'92t; maybe she\'92d just never encountered such obstacles that assaulted her capabilities before.\
The room closed in around Sarah. The garden clouded over. She blinked tears away.\
Hayley quietly slid off the sofa and sat on the floor with her legs curled under the coffee table, gathered a throw pillow to her chest and draped an arm over the sofa\'92s seat cushion. \'93Sarah?\'94\
Sarah turned, and remained resolutely by the windows. At that moment, Kathy slid into the room; she must\'92ve been just outside the door, out of sight, observing, waiting. She went to Sarah\'92s side and slid an arm around her waist.\
Sarah smiled weakly over her firm jaw; it looked a little maniacal.\
\'93Why don\'92t you come sit down?\'94 Kathy offered as she tried to guide Sarah.\
\'93I\'92d rather stand, thank you,\'94 Sarah said, tender yet firm. And somehow aristocratic. As Kathy accepted her response and sat herself down\'97seemingly without disturbing the air in the room\'97Sarah met Hayley\'92s gaze as she sat on the floor between the table and the sofa.\
\'93I had, in my possession, for a time, the \'91state secrets\'92 Agent Jones is so bent on recovering. But I surrendered the thing.\'94 Sarah shuddered.\
\'93The thing?\'94\
\'93It\'92s called a Gyges Disc,\'94 Sarah said.\
Kathy swallowed a pang of rejection; Sarah hadn\'92t told her any of this.\
Hayley asked, \'93Who did you surrender it to?\'94\
Sarah almost said, 
\f2\i Johnny
\f1\i0 , but checked herself. \'93Eloise.\'94 A flush of guilt came over her like a fast tide as she faced the truth of why she\'92d given it away. It wasn\'92t because she buckled under Eloise\'92s threats; she was just glad to be rid of the thing. It was only later that she began to consider the awful things someone like Eloise might do with it.\
Hayley thought for a moment. \'93Huh,\'94 she said, as if some theory pieces were falling into place. \'93How much did she want it?\'94\
Sarah chewed on that and swallowed. \'93She killed someone for it.\'94 Her eyes grew steely, and Hayley sensed she was finding some deep long-acquiescent resolve inside herself.\
\'93Perhaps she wanted it back,\'94 Hayley said, more for the benefit of her own theory-building than for driving the conversation with Sarah forward.\
\'93I should try to get it back,\'94 Sarah said, following her own separate line of reasoning.\
\'93Eloise has it, and perhaps always wanted it,\'94 Hayley offered to comfort Sarah.\
\'93It\'92s my fault she has it now,\'94 Sarah said to herself.\
\'93Before you had it, Tony somehow stumbled across it.\'94 Hayley said. \'93Before that, we know Agent Jones expected Travis Teh had it. If so, someone took it from him and staged his suicide.\'94 Had Eloise possessed it before that? Or had Teh acquired it for her, and subsequently lost it before handing it over?\
Kathy injected, her voice trembling. \'93You kept this thing\'97a thing people are willing to kill for\'97in the mansion for\'97what? A month? And you never told me?\'94\
Sarah saw the accusations in Kathy\'92s eyes, and felt fibres in her own heart rending. \'93I\'92m sorry\'85 I\'85 it\'92s such a vile thing, I decided I must keep it from\'85 everyone.\'94 And yet she\'92d surrendered it so easily to Eloise. Sarah set her jaw and resolved to correct that.\
\'93Vile?\'94 Kathy asked and searched Sarah\'92s face. She read the distress there, the tension in her neck. \'93Sarah, what did it do to you?\'94\
Sarah gulped and fought back the sensation of it, sticky and slavering, fusing into her skin, like it was some demon slug trying to take possession of her. She put her hand to her mouth to take back control over her urge to vomit.\
\'93Sarah what is it?\'94 Hayley prompted sombrely.\
Sarah tried to blink away visions of Tony\'92s torment at the hands of PAPER, and his unnatural ability to heal from the torture. That the gooey-disc-come-demonic-body-snatcher could bestow such powers upon Tony, was the more terrifying consequence for her. \'93Follow the earthquakes,\'94 she blurted as a diversion from those two things she must forever keep secret.\
\'93Sorry, what?\'94 Kathy asked.\
\'93It\'92s how Eloise knew I had it. She told me the disc was easy to track down\'85 just follow the earthquakes, she said.\'94\
\'93Earthquakes?\'94 Hayley sat up on the sofa again. \'93What earthquakes?\'94\
Kathy gestured Sarah over to come sit between her and Hayley. Sarah settled.\
\'93I hate garlic sauce!\'94 Sarah said, reaching for memories, hoping to make connections. \'93It was the Disc,\'94 she said.\
\'93What?\'94 Hayley asked. \'93What was the Disc?\'94\
\'93Tony.\'94 Sarah was about to say, 
\f2\i Tony lifted it from a dead guy in the street
\f1\i0 , and quickly checked herself. \'93We escaped from Tony\'92s apartment\'85\'94\
\'93This was after you subdued Agent Jones by clocking him with a case of beer?\'94 Hayley checked.\
Sarah regarded Hayley with a moment\'92s appraisal. \'93Yes. We escaped in Tony\'92s van. Not long after we had an accident. I thought we must have hit a pothole, which tipped the van on its side. We careened into a kebab cart.\'94\
\'93Garlic sauce!\'94 Kathy effused and clapped.\
Sarah smiled; Kathy always had a calming and disarming manner about her. \'93Yes, all over the window.\'94\
\'93So you said you thought you hit a pothole?\'94 Hayley asked.\
\'93Yeah\'85 but I went back and checked. There was no pothole\'85 well nothing big enough to throw a van like that.\'94\
\'93Then, what collided with the van?\'94\
\'93Just follow the earthquakes, Detective,\'94 Sarah answered, but it was more a statement of wonder than instruction.\
\'93You think there was an earthquake that tossed Chantara\'92s van and did absolutely no other damage.\'94\
\'93Well, you see The Gyges disc\'85 Tony had thrown it into the passenger-side footwell of his van, weeks before, and promptly forgot about it. I found it there that night.\'94\
\'93You just found it there?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93In the dark?\'94\
\'93It\'92s a very strange object, detective. Most of the time it just looks like a thick circular\'85 well, disc\'85 punched out of alligator hide, or something. But, just once that night, it kinda shimmered\'85\'94\
\'93Shimmered\'85\'94\
\'93Yes, like it had its own internal light source.\'94\
\'93I see. Go on.\'94\
\'93Well, that got my attention, so I picked it up\'85 examined it. It felt like some kind of stiff leather. I could flex it somewhat. Then I tossed it, like a coin. But I never caught it, Detective, because the van hit a non-existent pothole or invisible wombat or\'85 a gravity wave, or something.\'94\
\'93You\'92re saying tossing the disc causes\'85 what?\'94\
\'93Just follow the earthquakes, Detective,\'94 Sarah enunciated, growing short of patience.\
Hayley contemplated. \'93So, it\'92s a weapon?\'94\
Sarah shrugged. \'93Could be, though I dunno how you would harness it.\'94\
\'93Hmm, I bet Eloise does,\'94 Kathy interjected.\
Hayley appeared to be making order of things in her head. \'93This was on the way to the Memphis Mafia Bar?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93And you went to the bar with Chantara, voluntarily?\'94\
\'93Well, setting aside we were involuntarily fleeing a PAPER agent, yes.\'94\
\'93And did you meet anyone else while you were there?\'94\
\'93We were scared\'97\'94\
\'93You and Chantara.\'94\
\'93Yes. So I called a friend, someone in tech, who might be able to explain why PAPER was chasing us for this disc.\'94\
\'93This was Johnny Stoat?\'94\
Sarah was startled. How much of her life had Lanning pried into? \'93Yes,\'94 she admitted.\
\'93How long had you been going out with Mr Stoat?\'94\
Sarah laughed, deeply and cathartically. \'93Johnny? Oh no, never.\'94 She laughed more. Kathy joined in.\
\'93But you did attend karaoke evenings at Elvi bars together?\'94\
\'93Once. He was just my wing man. I enjoy drinking him under the table. What else has he told you?\'94\
\'93Frankly, Sarah, very little of any value. He claimed he protected you against the PAPER agents.\'94\
Sarah laughed even harder. \'93No, the Elvi did a perfectly good job of that while Johnny hid under the table.\'94\
\'93And Chantara?\'94\
\'93I seem to recall he\'92s handy with a pool cue.\'94\
\'93So you met with Mr Stoat and discussed the disc.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93And where was Chantara?\'94\
\'93I made him go sit at another table. Johnny would not have tolerated sitting with a Cloudborne.\'94\
\'93Did you show Mr Stoat the disc?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93And what did he have to say about it?\'94\
\'93Nothing useful. He found some ancient drawing from a site that looked three centuries out of date. He thought it might be Atlantis, Lemuria or some 1980s government health logo\'97he couldn\'92t tell.\'94 Sarah paused, remembering. \'93Then he wanted to spin it. I had to stop him.\'94\
\'93He wanted to toss it?\'94\
\'93He was going to run an experiment. He\'92d worked out I\'92d already tossed it\'97that something had happened. He asked me if I\'92d experienced time jumps, memory loss. Asked about earthquakes. When I told him about the van crash, he wanted to see for himself.\'94\
\'93Did he toss it?\'94\
\'93No. I got it back from him before he could.\'94 Sarah\'92s face darkened. \'93That\'92s when PAPER raided the bar.\'94
